            "A patient nursed in isolation on a Ghanaian ward becomes withdrawn, tearful, and says the staff avoid him and he feels like a prisoner. Nothing about his organism explains this; everything about his care does. The nurse's response is not to reassure from the doorway but to <strong>go in</strong>: sit down in PPE, explain again why the precautions exist and that they are about the organism and not about him, agree how often someone will come and keep to it, give him a means of calling (bell, phone, the student's number), provide a radio, a phone charger, newspapers, a window, and arrange visits by family and, if he wishes, a chaplain or imam. Depression, delirium in the older patient and non-adherence to treatment all rise in isolation; screening for them is part of the care plan.",
