            "A small number of infections change this. For <strong>viral haemorrhagic fever</strong> (Ebola, Marburg, Lassa) the body is highly infectious: it is handled only by a trained burial team in full PPE, placed in a sealed, chlorine-decontaminated double bag, not washed or dressed by the family, and buried promptly in a <strong>safe and dignified burial</strong> that still allows the family to be present at a distance and to perform what rites can be adapted. For <strong>cholera</strong> the body leaks infectious fluid and is disinfected and bagged, with funerals discouraged from involving food prepared by those who handled the body — funeral gatherings have seeded cholera outbreaks in Ghana, including the Greater Accra outbreak of 2024, which began after a funeral in Ada. For open <strong>tuberculosis</strong> there is no risk from the body itself unless the lungs are manipulated at post-mortem.",
            "The opposite error matters just as much. <strong>Dead bodies after a disaster do not cause epidemics</strong>: people killed by floods, fires or building collapse carry no more infection than they did alive, and the rush to mass burial causes years of anguish to families who never identified their dead and blocks inheritance and remarriage. Bodies are handled with dignity and gloves, identified and recorded, and buried individually where at all possible.",
