            "<strong>Assess dehydration before anything else</strong>: a weak or absent radial pulse, lethargy or unconsciousness, sunken eyes, inability to drink, a skin pinch that goes back very slowly and capillary refill over three seconds mean <strong>severe dehydration</strong> — intravenous <strong>Ringer's lactate</strong> immediately, 100 mL/kg over three hours in adults (30 mL/kg in the first 30 minutes, then 70 mL/kg over two and a half hours; over six hours in infants under one year), reassessed every 15–30 minutes until the pulse is strong, and oral rehydration solution started as soon as the patient can drink. The circulatory sign overrides the fact that he can still swallow. <strong>Some dehydration</strong> (restless, thirsty, sunken eyes, slow skin pinch) is treated with <strong>ORS 75 mL/kg over four hours</strong> under observation; <strong>no dehydration</strong> with ORS after every stool at home. Ongoing losses are replaced stool for stool; a cholera cot with a bucket beneath the hole lets you measure them. <strong>Zinc</strong> is given to children under five for 10–14 days; a single-dose antibiotic (<strong>doxycycline</strong> in adults; azithromycin in children and pregnant women, or according to the current national guideline and local sensitivity) shortens the illness and shedding in <strong>severe</strong> cases only — antibiotics are not the treatment, fluids are.",
